'''II.''' ''n.'' The logical doctrine of demonstration and of science.
{{smaller block|''Apodictic'', we may assume, is in like manner the formal study of what constitutes knowledge strictly so called, the nature of the principles on which knowledge rests, the special marks distinguishing it, and the method by which knowledge is framed.
{{right|''R. Adamson'', Encyc. Brit., XIV. 785.}}}}

'''apodictical, apodeictical''' (ap-ọ̄-dik′ti-kal, -dīk′ti-kal), ''a.'' Same as ''apodictic'', ''apodeictic.''

'''apodictically, apodeictically''' (ap-ọ̄-dik′ti-kạl-i, -dīk′ti-kal-i), ''adv.'' '''1.''' Demonstratively; so as to be evident beyond contradiction.
{{smaller block|Kant’s marvellous acuteness did not prevent his transcendental from being ''apodeictically'' resolved into absolute idealism.
{{right|''Sir W. Hamilton.''}}
''Apodictically'', we should say, if α is the cause of β, then all A which possesses α possesses β; thus reasoning from cause to causatum.
{{right|''R. Adamson'', Encyc. Brit., XIV. 789.}}}}

readings.c1s3:
{{smaller block|first clause is the ''protasis'', the second the ''apodosis''. When the protasis is introduced by such conditional conjunctions as ''notwithstanding, though, although'', the apodosis predicates something opposite to what might have been looked for: as, Although we were few in numbers (''protasis''), we overthrew the enemy (''apodosis''). By some grammarians the term is not restricted to conditional sentences, but is extended to others similarly constructed: thus, in a simile the apodosis is the application or latter part.}}
'''2.''' In the ''Gr. Ch.'', the last day of a church festival when prolonged throughout several days.
{{smaller block|It is sometimes coincident with or later than the octave, but generally earlier.}}

'''apodous''' (ap′ọ̄-dus), ''a.'' [< ''apod'' + ''-ous''.] Footless; apodal.

'''apodyterium''' (ap″ọ̄-di-tē′ri-um), ''n.''; pl. ''apodyteria'' (-ä̤). [L., < Gr. ἀποδυτήριον, < ἀποδύεσθαι, undress one’s self, mid. of ἀποδύειν, strip, undress, < ἀπό + δύειν, get into, put on.] An apartment in Greek and Roman baths, or in the palæstra, etc., where the bathers or those taking part in gymnastic exercises undressed and dressed.

'''apogæum†, apogeum†, apogæon†, apogeon†''' (ap-ọ̄-jē′um, -on), ''n.'' [ML., NL.: see ''apogee''.] Original forms of ''apogee''.
{{smaller block|Thy sun in his ''apogæon'' placed.
{{right|''Fairfax'', tr. of Tasso, ii. 67.}}
It is not yet agreed in what time, precisely, the ''apogeum'' absolveth one degree.
{{right|''Sir T. Browne'', Vulg. Err.}}}}

'''apogamic''' (ap-ọ̄-gam′ik), ''a.'' Same as ''apogamous''.
{{smaller block|The author could not detect any act of impregnation [in a parasite on the olive], and believes that reproduction is ''apogamic''.
{{right|''Jour. Roy. Micros. Soc.'', 2d ser., VI. 298.}}}}

